==English==
{{wikipedia|lang=en}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|grc|en}} {{m|grc|σκῶρ||excrement}} + {{m|en|-ology}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~|scatologies}}

# The [[scientific]] [[study]] or [[chemical]] [[analysis]] of [[faeces]].
# A [[filthy]] [[epithet]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2008|author=Daniel Bernardi|title=The Persistence of Whiteness|passage={{...}} lingo of the streets with its spewed out '''scatologies''' and its anti-womanist rhetoric of "hoes and bitches" &mdash; all so evocative of life in the ghetto {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|psychology|medicine}} Interest in or [[obsession]] with faeces or other [[excrement]].
#* {{quote-book|en|2015|Henry Powell and Howard Kushner|Music, Neurology, and Neuroscience||290|Like James Joyce’s Leopold Bloom in the novel ''Ulysses'' (1922), Mozart seemed utterly comfortable with himself, bodily functions, and all.{{...}} In our view Mozart’s so-called '''scatology''' is part of his culture and personality and, as we have argued, intimately connected with his creativity.|editors=Eckart Altenmüller, Stanley Finger, and François Boller|chapter=Mozart at play: The limitations of attributing etiology of genius to Tourette syndrome and mental illness}}
# [[literature|Literature]], [[humor]], or [[pornography]] featuring excrement or [[excrete|excreting]].
#* {{quote-book|en|2010|Julie Cross|Humor in Contemporary Junior Literature||48|The move to out-and-out '''scatology''' in humorous texts for junior readers takes off in the later 1990s, following the overt scatological humor of ground-breaking ‘bum and poo’ picture books for younger readers, such as Holzwarth’s ''The Little Mole Who Knew It Was None of His Business'' (1998).}}

